\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Example}
\title{The compact three-dimensional Spin group is the unit quaternions}

\p{Let #{Q_{3,0}(x_0,x_1,x_2)=x_0^2+x_1^2+x_2^2} and write #{e_0,e_1,e_2}
for its coordinate basis. TauCeti's chosen algebra equivalence
#{\theta:\Cl^{+}(Q_{3,0})\simeq\mathbb H} has the exact axes

##{e_0e_1\longmapsto-k,\qquad
e_0e_2\longmapsto i,\qquad
e_1e_2\longmapsto j}

\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.realCliffordThreeZeroEvenEquivQuaternion}
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.realCliffordThreeZeroEvenEquivQuaternion_ι_single_zero_one}
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.realCliffordThreeZeroEvenEquivQuaternion_ι_single_two_zero}
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.realCliffordThreeZeroEvenEquivQuaternion_ι_single_one_two}.
Hence

##{\theta(s+A e_0e_1+B e_0e_2+C e_1e_2)
=s+B i+C j-A k.}

Under #{\theta}, reversal is quaternion conjugation and reverse norm is

##{s^2+A^2+B^2+C^2}

\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.realCliffordThreeZeroEvenEquivQuaternion_reverseEven}
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.realCliffordThreeZeroEvenEquivQuaternion_map_reverseEven_mul_self_eq_normSq}.}

\p{Let #{\mathbb H^1=\{q\in\mathbb H:\bar q q=1\}}. Restricting #{\theta}
to Spin gives a homomorphism

##{\Spin(Q_{3,0})\longrightarrow\mathbb H^1.}

For #{q=r+x i+y j+z k\in\mathbb H^1}, the inverse Clifford value is

##{r-z e_0e_1+x e_0e_2+y e_1e_2.}

Its reverse norm is one, so the dimension-three carrier lemma places it in
the actual Spin group. The algebra inverse proves both compositions and
multiplicativity. Therefore

##{\Spin(Q_{3,0})\simeq\mathbb H^1.}

TauCeti separately packages the algebra map as an equivalence between the
even-unitary carrier and unit quaternions
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.realCliffordThreeZeroEvenUnitaryEquivQuaternionUnitary};
the preceding carrier lemma is what identifies that carrier with Spin in
this real dimension-three case.}

\p{In \citet{Section 1.4, pp. 28--29}{gallier2014clifford}, the analogous
compact calculation writes the even element, obtains its sum-of-four-squares
norm, and identifies the unit quaternions. Its generators square to #{-1}.
Likewise, \citet{I.3, Theorem 3.7, pp. 23--24; I.4, (4.0), p. 25}{lawson2016spin}
uses #{v^2=-q(v)} to reduce an even algebra and identify the quaternion base
entry. Translating by #{Q=-q} and the even sign-change equivalence gives the
same compact algebra and norm. The displayed axis signs are the exact
TauCeti choice above, rather than a relabeling imported from either source.}
